+  "problem": "\ud83e\udde0 **ARCHI (BARCHI-3)** \u2014 the id sweep misses ids that exist only in an unlanded tree; make the open-PR file list part of the protocol\n\n## Observed\n\nOn 2026-08-30 two seats independently allocated **`WP-2026-0110`** and **`BADF-DEM-0097`** within the hour (#224 and #226). Both PRs wrote `work/WP-2026-0110/` and `badf/demands/BADF-DEM-0097.json` with **different content**; whichever landed second would have silently overwritten the other's work-package record, G07 gate dossier, composition record, build ledger and authority anchor. Caught by BADF-QA before either merged.\n\nThe existing convention (`main` + every remote head + issues/PR titles \u2014 the `BADF-DEM-0065` lesson of 2026-08-29) is real and was followed. It is not sufficient: **a work package's id lives in `work/<WP>/` and `badf/demands/` on a branch that may be pushed but unlanded, and it appears in no title, no body and no `main` tree.** The high-water sweep returns the last *landed* id and hands out one that is already bound to a dossier somewhere.\n\n## Expected \u2014 add a fourth surface\n\n```bash\nfor pr in $(gh pr list --state open --json number --jq '.[].number'); do\n  gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/$pr/files --jq '.[].filename'\ndone | grep -oE 'WP-2026-[0-9]{4}|DEM-[0-9]{4}' | sort -u\n```\n\nRun alongside the three existing surfaces. It catches exactly the class that collided here \u2014 an id bound in a pushed-but-unmerged tree \u2014 and it is the cheapest of the four.\n\n**It still does not see everything, and the gap is not closable by tooling:** ids claimed in a *local, unpushed* worktree are invisible to every remote query. That is why the **ask-the-other-seats half of measure-then-ask is not a courtesy** \u2014 it is the only instrument that sees unpushed work. Both halves are load-bearing and neither substitutes for the other.\n\n## Why this is worth a control rather than lore\n\n`main` (and even `main` + heads) is a **proxy** for \"every claimed